WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:08.600
Irradiate a budding yeast cell that lacks a gene called RAD9.

2
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.220
It does not pause.

3
00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:14.320
It keeps dividing for several generations, and it dies.

4
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:20.260
Hold the same cell back with a drug for a few hours, and it repairs the damage.

5
00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.740
So what was the gene for?

6
00:00:23.633 --> 00:00:26.533
RAD9 was not a repair gene.

7
00:00:27.113 --> 00:00:28.713
It was a gene for waiting.

8
00:00:29.833 --> 00:00:33.073
Hartwell and Weinert named such a control a checkpoint.

9
00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:37.673
A checkpoint makes one cell cycle event wait for another.

10
00:00:38.493 --> 00:00:43.413
Remove it, and the cell still divides, but on time instead of on completion.

11
00:00:44.383 --> 00:00:51.243
Before anaphase, every pair of sister chromatids must attach to opposite poles, at a

12
00:00:51.243 --> 00:00:52.843
site called the kinetochore.

13
00:00:53.803 --> 00:00:58.583
In a PtK1 cell, one chromosome was attached to only one pole.

14
00:00:59.823 --> 00:01:04.583
Rieder's group destroyed its unattached kinetochore with a laser, and anaphase

15
00:01:04.583 --> 00:01:06.283
followed about 20 minutes later.

16
00:01:06.983 --> 00:01:09.043
That chromosome never reached the middle.

17
00:01:09.283 --> 00:01:13.103
So the wait signal comes from an unattached kinetochore.

18
00:01:13.933 --> 00:01:20.553
Unattached kinetochores promote assembly of the mitotic checkpoint complex, made of

19
00:01:20.553 --> 00:01:25.713
Mad2, Cdc20, BubR1 and Bub3.

20
00:01:27.193 --> 00:01:33.913
Cdc20 is the activator that the APC/C ligase needs to pick its mitotic

21
00:01:33.913 --> 00:01:40.673
targets. BubR1 carries sequences that mimic a target's binding sequence, and they

22
00:01:40.673 --> 00:01:43.333
sit in the binding sites of Cdc20.

23
00:01:44.193 --> 00:01:46.633
It is a key broken off in the lock.

24
00:01:47.617 --> 00:01:52.877
When the last kinetochore attaches, the checkpoint complex releases its grip.

25
00:01:53.977 --> 00:02:00.997
APC/C with Cdc20 ubiquitinates securin and cyclin B, and the proteasome

26
00:02:00.997 --> 00:02:06.377
destroys them. Securin had held separase, a protease, inactive.

27
00:02:07.597 --> 00:02:13.077
Freed, separase cuts Scc1, the cohesin that holds sister chromatids together.

28
00:02:13.077 --> 00:02:14.697
Anaphase begins.

29
00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:19.650
Here is a made-up time course for securin in HeLa cells.

30
00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:27.470
After metaphase, it falls from 100 percent to 50 in 10 minutes, so its half-life is 10

31
00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:33.190
minutes. Add a spindle poison at 10 minutes, and the fall stops near 50.

32
00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:39.690
A phosphate can be removed in seconds, but a destroyed protein must be made again.

33
00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:45.430
So a checkpoint makes the cell wait, and destruction makes the move one way.
